  "worked_solution": "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0,$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)}={e}^{-as}F(s),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=L{f(t)}.$\n\nIn our case, we wish to find the transform of\n\n$$\nu(t-4){e}^{-5(t-4)}.\n$$\n\nBy comparing this expression to the form in the theorem, we can identify the following:\n\n- The shift is $a=4.$\n\n- The shifted function is $f(t-4)={e}^{-5(t-4)}.$\n\nFrom the shifted function, we can deduce the original unshifted function $f(t)$ by simply replacing $(t-4)$ with $t:$\n\n$$\nf(t)={e}^{-5t}.\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the Laplace transform $F(s)$ of the unshifted function:\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{{e}^{-5t}}=\\frac{1}{s-(-5)}=\\frac{1}{s+5}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the shifting theorem formula $L{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)}={e}^{-as}F(s)$ by multiplying $F(s)$ by the exponential delay factor ${e}^{-4s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-4){e}^{-5(t-4)}} & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
